WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:03.409
- [Voiceover] Anonymous,
you've probably heard of them.

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:05.721
They're the group of online
activists that carry out cyber ops

00:00:05.721 --> 00:00:08.215
to advance social and political change.

00:00:08.215 --> 00:00:11.247
And sometimes, they need a lawyer,

00:00:11.247 --> 00:00:12.997
a really good lawyer.

00:00:15.728 --> 00:00:18.642
At first glance, Jay Leiderman
seems like a regular guy.

00:00:18.642 --> 00:00:20.745
He lives in the suburbs,
drives a fast car,

00:00:20.745 --> 00:00:23.188
and is a self-professed Deadhead.

00:00:23.188 --> 00:00:25.914
Oh, and he loves whisky.

00:00:25.914 --> 00:00:26.935
But there's more to Jay's story

00:00:26.935 --> 00:00:28.982
than immediately meets the eye.

00:00:28.982 --> 00:00:30.518
When Jay sits down at the computer,

00:00:30.518 --> 00:00:32.397
he's anything but average.

00:00:32.397 --> 00:00:35.140
- I represent accused cyber criminals,

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.216
many of whom are either
affiliated with Anonymous

00:00:38.216 --> 00:00:40.383
or closely related groups.

00:00:41.376 --> 00:00:44.207
- [Voiceover] And it all
started with a tweet.

00:00:44.207 --> 00:00:45.703
- [Jay] I sent out a couple tweets

00:00:45.703 --> 00:00:46.988
giving attention to the fact

00:00:46.988 --> 00:00:48.917
that I would take a pro bono case.

00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:49.750
The next morning,

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.831
there was an email in my
inbox from Commander X.

00:00:52.831 --> 00:00:54.513
- [Voiceover] Commander
X, a prominent member

00:00:54.513 --> 00:00:56.683
of the hacktivist group Anonymous.

00:00:56.683 --> 00:00:59.742
- Commander X was the first
hacktivist I represented.

00:00:59.742 --> 00:01:02.905
Once he felt that I was his lawyer,

00:01:02.905 --> 00:01:05.441
he determined unilaterally

00:01:05.441 --> 00:01:08.349
that I was the lawyer for Anonymous.

00:01:08.349 --> 00:01:12.231
There are a number of
encrypted chat-type programs

00:01:12.231 --> 00:01:13.976
that I would prefer to use.

00:01:13.976 --> 00:01:15.620
I like to keep the NSA guessing

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.894
with which method of
communication I'm going to use

00:01:18.894 --> 00:01:19.870
with the idea that perhaps

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:22.579
they can't monitor them all at once.

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:25.904
When someone contacts me, and
I do not know who they are,

00:01:25.904 --> 00:01:29.915
it's the most beautiful,
purest form of conversation,

00:01:29.915 --> 00:01:33.055
because there is absolutely
no judgement on anything.

00:01:33.055 --> 00:01:36.401
Over the computer, everyone is equal.

00:01:36.401 --> 00:01:37.234
- [Voiceover] One of the hardest things

00:01:37.234 --> 00:01:38.268
about representing Anonymous

00:01:38.268 --> 00:01:39.507
is working around digital laws

00:01:39.507 --> 00:01:41.457
that were written decades ago.

00:01:41.457 --> 00:01:43.290
His main gripe is with the CFAA,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.512
the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act.

00:01:45.512 --> 00:01:47.145
- The penalties are outrageous.

00:01:47.145 --> 00:01:49.510
It's a bad law. It's a broken law.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:52.882
It's not something that fits
with today's technology.

00:01:52.882 --> 00:01:55.453
Sharing your Netflix
password with your friends,

00:01:55.453 --> 00:01:57.746
that could be a federal crime.

00:01:57.746 --> 00:02:00.476
I hope that by helping hacktivists,

00:02:00.476 --> 00:02:03.237
by pursuing these cases in the courts,

00:02:03.237 --> 00:02:06.979
by continuing to keep the
issues alive in the media,

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:09.567
that we can enact some degree of change.

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:11.291
I hope it's not just a few of us crazies

00:02:11.291 --> 00:02:12.124
running through the wilderness.

00:02:12.124 --> 00:02:14.541
(soft music)

